#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120648} :activity/id #uuid "8734274c-15b6-589a-a77f-6fd025ab063a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049788 :topic/id #uuid "1420f0fa-c9b8-5cba-8bba-e14cbf53c041" :topic/math-academy-id 1776} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121026 :tutorial/content "Cramer's rule applies to square systems of linear equations with any number of variables. In this lesson, we will practice applying Cramer's rule to solve $3 \\times 3$ systems of equations.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y+{a}_{13}z={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y+{a}_{23}z={b}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{31}x+{a}_{32}y+{a}_{33}z={b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/39/39c4a39573975acc249a4402799f40ef8d72666d4da03d6c4ed6cc299bc7fc52.svg). \nThen Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y,z \\right> = \\left\\langle  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\dfrac{D_y}{D}, \\dfrac{D_z}{D} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n\n![](images/dc/dcda1374b4582e7978133bbf3cea0375b40095d353573f826a3bac7f212dfc03.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f245bf99-3b45-5a27-9aca-aaee391628ed" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3079 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "620dc976-5ed3-54d8-a37a-7483259c65bb" :step/math-academy-id 16705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120706}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119789 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119600 :question/id #uuid "41a8466d-8bc6-5811-a557-e5a4e840f51c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4577"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554570}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "c34a23fc-50c9-5fb1-b89d-0a53b98720ed" :step/math-academy-id 16707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119791 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119602 :question/id #uuid "e1eb00d6-77fe-5110-8095-a54e63981c09" :question/math-academy-id "e-4579"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554592}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "bd4aa514-64cb-5bde-8bf4-8e5364299dc3" :step/math-academy-id 16711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121047 :tutorial/content "To demonstrate using Cramer's rule on a $3 \\times 3$ system, consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y+z=9 \\\\ 2y+3z=7 \\\\ -8x+y+6z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y+1z=9 \\\\ 0x+2y+3z=7 \\\\ -8x+1y+6z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(12-3)-2(0+24)+(0+16) \\\\  & =36-48+16 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $D_x,$ $D_y,$ and $D_z.$\n\n- To find $D_x,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *first* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9(12-3)-2(42-0)+(7-0) \\\\  & =81-84+7 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find $D_y,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *second* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|-9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(42)-9(0+24)+(0+56) \\\\  & =168-216+56 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find $D_z,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}|+9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-7)-2(0+56)+9(0+16) \\\\  & =-28-112+144 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩ & =⟨\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{4},\\frac{8}{4},\\frac{4}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9d916943-4be4-554c-a4f5-51aef55e8254" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3241 :tutorial/title "A Demonstration of Cramer's Rule on a 3x3 Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "e9ce92e1-dc54-5375-b685-ec0fb5099a5c" :step/math-academy-id 17835 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119852 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119667 :question/id #uuid "33693f3b-713f-5ffc-bd4e-27ad7a1e0c7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6172"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050129}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554598}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "d5e91e09-6b2c-5a23-a95e-c5b4f8576a52" :step/math-academy-id 23061 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120649}}] :activity/title "Cramer’s Rule for 3x3 Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119998}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416307} :activity/id #uuid "71b4c62d-258c-5e6d-b466-361c4a8360c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049788 :topic/id #uuid "1420f0fa-c9b8-5cba-8bba-e14cbf53c041" :topic/math-academy-id 1776} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a43538-527f-5e1c-8777-5a1b965cf68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416275} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5a75d9-2669-5164-ae35-761e45c73242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416277} {:answer/id #uuid "630877d4-1651-51d8-aadc-515dcd79b003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416282} {:answer/id #uuid "8103e5b5-a679-5861-8dd6-741437678c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416285} {:answer/id #uuid "f73e832f-6af7-5391-9436-80207cb6c290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416277} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d561005-378c-508e-a82e-2d8043716be1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9a1ee2de-30e9-5dde-87e2-a6069fa7a03b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73695" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+z &=-1 \\\\ x+5y+3z &=k \\\\ 7x-3y-z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $z=-3$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $-16,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|=-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $z$ is given by $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & k \\\\ 7 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-(4-7k)-(-3-35) \\\\  & =-4+7k+38 \\\\  & =7k+34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{7k+34}{-16}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $z=-3.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7k+34}{-16} & =-3 \\\\ 7k+34 & =48 \\\\ 7k & =14 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d86e120-ea1d-5a4e-b49e-e1648f92cfdc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0520e7af-36b0-5ef0-9563-eb7367b7cb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416272} {:answer/id #uuid "3675aea5-a0c8-57f7-93a0-b11b85ed8248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416278} {:answer/id #uuid "49e9c67b-3d28-59fc-80d1-1d4cc0713555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416280} {:answer/id #uuid "81135cf6-e842-57e1-aef8-392ad77737ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416286} {:answer/id #uuid "e67665d0-ea97-55a9-9c9d-1d7010759bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416280} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a46aaec-c335-5f1e-884c-19d1799ece0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "22790615-5765-50ce-b96c-1d82231be30c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42668" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be a unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y+z &=-4 \\\\ y-2z &=2 \\\\ 3x+2y+kz &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that  $y=6$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $7+4k,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=7+4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & k\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|-(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(2k+2)+4(0+6)+(0-6) \\\\  & =8k+8+24-6 \\\\  & =26+8k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{26+8k}{7+4k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $y=6.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{26+8k}{7+4k} & =6 \\\\ 26+8k & =42+24k \\\\ 16k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "21ebd4e5-a3bf-5e30-9a8d-944803219307" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fff04a-2bbe-5b26-ad65-edbd694ba9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416276} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85483-816c-510e-9fd1-dd20c0b4191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416284} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d8e391-d9ee-52da-94f1-37df36eceaa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416289} {:answer/id #uuid "c108d1a2-60fc-5674-aa0a-d546a2379dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416291} {:answer/id #uuid "ee89842c-a371-5c4d-bdf4-656e2b69eca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416289} :answer-field/id #uuid "432aa278-91b8-5c39-89e2-129966c0aa56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c8205961-c58e-5aea-a7e2-15eb2dd17972" :question/math-academy-id "q-43115" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3z &=7 \\\\ kx-4y+z &=-19 \\\\ x-y-4z &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that  $z=-2$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $7k+23,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ k & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7k+23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $z$ is given by $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 7 \\\\ k & -4 & -19 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & -19 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -19 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-16-19)-(4k+19)+7(-k+4) \\\\  & =-70-4k-19-7k+28 \\\\  & =-61-11k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-61-11k}{7k+23}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $z=-2.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-61-11k}{7k+23} & =-2 \\\\ -61-11k & =-14k-46 \\\\ 3k & =15 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3de30988-2509-57c4-b0bc-4b6cac063024" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e0253b-532c-58d8-b091-347993c3f893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416273} {:answer/id #uuid "061ca3b9-19da-5830-8387-482e73317df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416274} {:answer/id #uuid "489fa8d1-175b-5fa4-8798-bbe1b2637681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416279} {:answer/id #uuid "73c24e43-321d-50e6-b1e7-cdf2748817be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416283} {:answer/id #uuid "b917f57a-83ba-5e72-bb47-0cffe8883bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416274} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc965890-90b8-58cc-bc3f-efeb4ad8684c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2a3030e8-5052-55cf-b8fe-d458398f5913" :question/math-academy-id "q-73691" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3y &=1 \\\\ 5x+4y-2z &=0 \\\\ y-z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $-21,$ then $y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+(-3)y+0z &=1 \\\\ 5x+4y+(-2)z &=0 \\\\ 0x+1y+(-1)z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{y},$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the second column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(0+6)-(-5-0)+0 \\\\  & =18+5 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{23}{-21}=-\\frac{23}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4748b1d1-3271-5818-be4b-23693a0b5577" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc23eff-2e85-53ab-85d6-24718d451428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416281} {:answer/id #uuid "a30ac57b-8a8c-5c62-8fa8-59e9b44f8567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416287} {:answer/id #uuid "a379347f-b3fd-507c-a103-9d1da21b2d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416288} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8728d4-e8d5-5f53-8f10-8aeddf36b55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416292} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a3b796-b620-52fb-a517-36825c287eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416287} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f143de8-4808-52ba-8787-6ee60a482eb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a38bcb4-0b4c-5b8b-bb9c-b7e3ce5c912c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42673" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y &=-3 \\\\ x-3y-2z &=4 \\\\ -2x+y+2z &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $-14,$ then $z=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+1y+0z &=-3 \\\\ 1x+(-3)y+(-2)z &=4 \\\\ (-2)x+1y+2z &=-5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}|+(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(15-4)-(-5+8)-3(1-6) \\\\  & =44-3+15 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{56}{-14}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e8d31d5-c990-5339-ae25-b77f962093ed" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416308}}] :activity/title "Cramer’s Rule for 3x3 Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416271}]}
